# .trivyignore
#
# CVEs accepted for the published fetchly image, consumed by the Trivy scan in
# .github/workflows/docker-build.yml. The scan already passes --ignore-unfixed,
# so anything listed here is a finding that HAS a fix available and is being
# accepted anyway - record why, and revisit it on the next release.
#
# Format: one vulnerability id per line, with the reason in a comment above it.
#
#   # torch pulls this transitively; not reachable from any code path fetchly
#   # executes, upstream fix expected in <version>.
#   CVE-0000-00000

# The two below are both reported against pip's *vendored* copies, not against
# anything installed in the image. pip 26.2.1 ships PEP 770 SBOM documents in
# its .dist-info/sboms/, Trivy reads them (hence the "Third-party SBOM may lead
# to inaccurate vulnerability detection" warnings in the scan log) and reports
# the versions bundled inside pip/_vendor/. They carry no FilePath in the
# report, and each appears twice - once for /usr/local/lib/python3.13 pip and
# once for the /venv one.
#
# Nothing in the running container executes pip, and the packages that ARE
# installed are already past both fixes: the venv carries setuptools 84.0.0,
# and msgpack is not a dependency of this application at all. Re-check when
# pip refreshes its vendored bundle - dropping these entries then is the
# expected outcome, not carrying them forward.

# pip vendors msgpack for CacheControl's HTTP cache serializer. Only reachable
# by running pip, which the image never does at runtime.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# pip's vendor.txt pins setuptools for pkg_resources. The CVE is in
# setuptools' PackageIndex.download path handling, which needs pip's own
# package-index code path to be executed.
CVE-2025-47273
